From 57abd06e7e2c287eb1e7113c8bed14551e0c9c16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emjupy <dev@example.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 19:06:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add split (C-c s) and merge-above (C-c m); harden the XSRF
 harvest

Split divides the source at point: text before stays, text from point on
moves to a new cell of the same type below, and point follows it there.
Output stays with the top half.

Merge joins this cell into the one above with a newline between them and
leaves point at the seam. The output of BOTH cells is discarded along
with their execution counts: neither set was produced by the merged
source, and keeping one would attribute results to code that never
generated them. It refuses on the first cell and across cell types,
where merging would silently reinterpret one of them.

Both use a new emjupy--cell-at-point that finds the cell from the
overlays instead of the emjupy-cell text property. The property is
stamped once at render time and plain insert does not carry it onto new
text, so anything typed at a cell boundary -- the end of its last line,
the obvious place to type -- was invisible to the lookup. That failed
loudly in some commands and silently in others: insert-cell-below found
no current cell and appended to the end of the notebook. Every lookup
site in emjupy-cells.el and emjupy-kernel.el now uses it.

The 403 had a second cause beyond the /api one already fixed. On a
server that wants credentials, /tree answers 302 to /login and the
redirect itself carries no cookie -- so the harvest came back empty and
every write was answered "'_xsrf' argument missing from POST". Measured
against a live server: /tree returns 302 with no Set-Cookie, /login
returns it directly. /login is now tried first, and redirects are no
longer chased, so each endpoint is asked on its own.

The cookie is also sent as an _xsrf query argument on writes, not just
as X-XSRFToken. Tornado's check reads either, and "argument missing" is
what it says when it finds neither, so sending both makes that message
impossible whenever a cookie is held at all.
